.TH mkfs 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
mkfs \- make a filesystem
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mkfs
[\fB\-f\fR] \fIdevice inodes blocks\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mkfs
writes an empty FUZIX filesystem onto
.IR device .
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I inodes
How many files the filesystem can ever hold.  This is a
.I count of inodes,
not a number of blocks to give them, and it cannot be changed
afterwards.  A filesystem that runs out of inodes reports itself full
with blocks still free, so do not be mean with it: about one inode per
64 blocks is a reasonable rule, and 2048 a sensible floor.
.TP
.I blocks
The size in 512-byte blocks.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-f
Fast: clear only the inode blocks rather than every block on the device.
The filesystem is just as correct; the difference is that old data
remains in the blocks that have not been allocated yet.  On a large card
this turns minutes into seconds.
.SH NOTES
This destroys whatever was on the device.  It does not ask.
.PP
The filesystem may fill its partition exactly: the FS32 format has no
unreachable sector at the end that must be kept clear.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# mkfs /dev/hda3 2048 131072
# mkfs -f /dev/hda3 2048 131072
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fsck (1),
.BR mount (1),
.BR fdisk (1),
.BR df (1)
